WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.580
The first trailer for Thor: Ragnarok has dropped,
and for less than two minutes of footage,

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:08.250
it’s positively loaded with intel on Marvel’s
latest project.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:12.120
From how the Incredible Hulk fits into all
this, to the new big bad who looks to take

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.950
out all of Asgard on her own, here’s everything
we learned in the first Thor: Ragnarok trailer

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:21.160
— and why we think the film will almost
certainly blow us all away when it drops on

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:21.980
November 3rd.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.120
Hela is hella powerful

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:29.400
Outside of Loki, villains have almost always
been the weak point across the MCU.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:33.750
After the fairly boring Dark Elves in The
Dark World, Marvel is looking to up the ante

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.100
in a big way with the villain in Ragnarok.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:40.800
Cate Blanchett is playing Marvel's Norse goddess
Hela — and she more than lives up to the

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:41.300
title.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.660
We get our first look at Hela here, and she
is positively terrifying.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.880
Blanchett looks plenty intimidating in the
role, as Thor and the full might of Asgard

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:52.240
looks to be no match for her destructive capabilities.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.140
Mjolnir destroyed

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:59.579
Thor’s weapon of choice has been a key piece
of the MCU since we first got a peek at Coulson

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:03.040
and his S.H.I.E.L.D. team trying to mine it
out of the ground in Iron Man 2’s post-credit

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:03.540
scene.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.280
But, all of that ends here.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.180
Thor tries to take on Hela with a trademark
throw of Mjolnir.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:11.000
But, she’s powerful enough to stop it midair.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:11.760
Worse?

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:17.080
She also crushes it into charred little hammer
bits, meaning Thor is officially hammer-less.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:18.380
No more hammer time, y’all.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:19.120
"Stop.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:20.140
Hammer time!"

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.420
Considering the big deal that’s been made
about this hammer, and how no one can lift

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:23.920
it...

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:28.500
Or in this case, hold it, without being worthy,
it makes one wonder just how powerful Hela

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:29.540
might actually be.

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:34.120
That hammer has always been one of the most
powerful objects in the MCU, and it was just

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.280
turned into a scrap heap.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:37.520
“Asgard is dead”

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:43.060
We see Hela face off with the whole of Asgard’s
army on her own, shortly before fire engulfs

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:43.980
the entire city.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.580
Hela makes the point even clearer with her
line:

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.200
“Asgard is dead.”

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:53.640
We’ve seen Asgard face its fair share of
damage in other Thor films, but we’ve never

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:56.360
seen the city of the gods actually wiped out.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:00.440
The events here could have a lasting impact
on the MCU, especially if it means there are

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.840
a lot of displaced Norse gods just roaming
the galaxy.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:04.280
The new faces

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:08.939
From Robert Redford in Captain America: The
Winter Solider, to Glenn Close in Guardians

00:02:08.939 --> 00:02:13.690
of the Galaxy, the MCU has been attracting
its fair share of A-list actors over the past

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:14.690
few years.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:18.739
Now, it’s time for Jurassic Park alum Jeff
Goldblum to make the jump into the Marvel

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:19.240
universe.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:23.680
Here, Goldblum plays the Grandmaster, who
seems to be pulling the strings of the super-powered

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.840
gladiatorial games where Thor finds himself
after being captured.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.740
Goldblum looks amazing in his costume, and
this looks to be one of the most colorful

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:31.720
Marvel films to date.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:39.420
The footage here also gives us our first good look at Creed alum Tessa Thompson, an Asgardian warrior who seems to be working for the Grandmaster.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:43.969
Valkyrie will serve as a potential love interest
for Thor, with Natalie Portman’s Jane Foster

00:02:43.969 --> 00:02:44.520
off the board.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.519
Judging by what we see of her here, it’ll
be interesting to see how her allegiances

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:49.760
are tested along the way.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:54.080
And finally, Karl Urban takes on the role
of Skurge, the Executioner, in one of the

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.800
coolest shoutouts to Thor's comic book past
that we've seen on screen.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:02.939
When you see this...the filmmakers are reminding
you of The Mighty Thor #362.

00:03:02.939 --> 00:03:07.419
In that issue, which comes from the legendary
story arc by Walt Simonson in the '80s, the

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:12.300
Executioner holds off the hordes of Hel...with
a couple of machine guns and his pure, unfiltered

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:12.800
rage.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:17.060
Karl Urban's already a comics-to-film legend
for his role in Dredd.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:20.980
Chances seem good he'll go down in history
for doing the Executioner justice, too.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:22.340
Loki's back

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:27.780
When last we left Loki, he was the secret
king of Asgard, having assumed Odin’s form

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:28.999
at the end of The Dark World.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.800
But, we see him back in his Tom Hiddleston
face here, wielding a few knives during a

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:33.300
fight scene.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:38.100
Loki shows up a bit later sitting with the
Grandmaster and watching Thor’s fight with the Hulk.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:41.579
So, is Loki pulling the strings to keep Thor
locked up?

00:03:41.579 --> 00:03:42.799
Is he there to rescue him?

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.189
Is he just there to enjoy the show?

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:45.860
We can’t wait to find out.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:47.650
Heimdall goes rogue

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:52.959
Idris Elba’s Heimdall has been a quiet force
in all the Thor movies, guarding the Bifrost

00:03:52.959 --> 00:03:54.749
and generally being a legit badass.

00:03:54.749 --> 00:03:58.940
But, he loses the fancy armor and finds himself
flying solo in Ragnarok.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:03.620
The brief shot of Heimdall we see here shows
him looking very much like scruffy Idris Elba,

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:07.200
but he’s still wielding that sword and mowing
down bad guys as needed.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:11.790
We’ve never really seem Heimdall out on
his own, seemingly without a Bifrost to defend.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:14.880
Will he eventually join up with Thor to try
and retake Asgard?

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:17.120
Thor and Hulk reunited

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.260
As expected, the best moment is when we finally
get a look at Mark Ruffalo’s Hulk in full-on

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:26.240
World War Hulk attire, ready to take on all
comers in the gladiator ring.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:29.200
“Yess!"

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:31.220
Yeah, we felt the same way, Thor.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:35.500
We still don’t know exactly how Hulk ended
up in deep space, or why he seems keen

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.710
to start wailing on Thor, but this is the
first set-up of the weirdest buddy movie we

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:41.220
never knew we wanted to see.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:43.240
They borrowed Star-Lord’s walkman

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:48.319
Guardians of the Galaxy set the tone for the
MCU in regards to how popular music can be

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:53.100
used to sell a story, and it seems Thor: Ragnarok
borrowed a cassette or two from Star-Lord’s

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:53.600
tape deck.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.520
The entire trailer is set to the rocking beats
of Led Zeppelin’s “Immigrant Song,”

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:01.639
and combined with the neon colors and font
book straight out of Star Trek II: The Wrath

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:05.200
of Khan, it sets a tone unlike any Thor movie
we’ve seen before.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.160
And it looks like the most fun they’ve had
in Asgard in ages.

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:11.000
Well, you know, except for all the destruction
and stuff.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:12.380
Thanks for watching!

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:15.160
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.720
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

